🛡️ Admin Dashboard

The administrator dashboard is intended for authorized application management.

Administrative Metrics
Total registered users.
Total resume analyses.
Recent registrations.
Open support tickets.
Analysis activity over time.
Frequently analyzed job categories.
User Management

Administrators can search users, filter results, inspect permitted account information, and disable or re-enable accounts.

Account-status changes should be recorded in an audit log.

Administrators must not receive unnecessary access to private resume contents.

Support Management

Administrators can:

View support tickets.
Filter by priority and status.
Assign tickets.
Reply to users.
Update ticket status.
Review relevant administrative activity.
FAQ Management

Administrators can create, edit, publish, unpublish, and delete FAQ entries, subject to their permissions.

Audit Logs

Important administrative events should record:

Administrator ID.
Action.
Target resource.
Timestamp.
Outcome.

Logs must not contain passwords, access tokens, complete resume text, or unnecessary sensitive information.

Administrative Security

The backend must enforce administrator permissions on every protected route. Hiding controls in the frontend is not sufficient security.

🔒 Security and Privacy

ResumeIQ handles potentially sensitive personal information and should follow secure development practices.

Required Security Controls
Argon2id password hashing.
Secure session and token handling.
Refresh-token rotation and revocation.
Role-based authorization.
Ownership checks for private resources.
Pydantic request validation.
SQLAlchemy parameterized database operations.
File-signature and format validation.
Upload-size restrictions.
Private file storage.
Request rate limiting.
Restricted CORS configuration.
CSRF protection where required.
HTTPS in production.
Secure HTTP headers.
Safe error responses.
Audit logging for sensitive administrative actions.
Secrets managed outside source control.
Dependency vulnerability checks.
Data-retention and deletion controls.
Threats to Address

The implementation should protect against:

SQL injection.
Cross-site scripting.
Cross-site request forgery.
Broken access control.
Insecure direct object references.
Brute-force attacks.
Malicious file uploads.
Path traversal.
Token theft.
Privilege escalation.
Sensitive-data exposure.
Resource-exhaustion attacks.
Resume Privacy

Resume files should be stored outside publicly accessible directories and should not be executed.

The system should:

Restrict access to the file owner and authorized services.
Avoid retaining original files longer than necessary.
Remove temporary files after processing.
Provide deletion controls.
Avoid logging complete resume contents.
Separate users' private data.
Apply an explicit chatbot conversation-retention policy.

Uploaded documents are untrusted input. Instructions embedded in a resume must not override application security rules or grant access to private information.

